  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-IP24-TZA102</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened women and youth entrepreneurship development policies and programmes especially in rural economy to create more decent work and a just transition to environmental sustainability</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The International Labour Organization (ILO) supports Tanzania in strengthening entrepreneurship development policies and programs for women and youth, particularly in rural areas, to create decent work opportunities and foster a just transition to environmental sustainability. The initiative focuses on promoting the transition of workers and enterprises from the informal to the formal economy by leveraging digital transformation to enhance productivity, access to social protection, and inclusion in financial services.

Recognizing that women and youth dominate informal employment in rural Tanzania, where vulnerabilities to social and economic shocks are high, this program seeks to address decent work deficits while fostering innovation and competitiveness. Through targeted training, capacity building, and partnerships, the program empowers women and youth entrepreneurs with skills, tools, and access to finance, enabling them to improve their livelihoods and contribute to sustainable economic growth.

Aligned with Tanzania&#x2019;s Development Vision 2025 and the National Five-Year Development Plan, this intervention also supports the Sustainable Development Goals (SDG) 8 on decent work and economic growth and SDG 13 on climate action. Key activities include promoting formalization, enhancing labor protections for excluded groups, and advancing green business practices in collaboration with public and private stakeholders. By fostering inclusive growth, the program contributes to poverty reduction, gender equality, and sustainable development in Tanzania.</narrative>
  </description>
